{"page":361,"blocks":[{"x":0.49187,"y":0.05257,"w":0.45964,"h":0.01535,"fs":0.01535,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"5.6 Definite Integral Substitutions and the Area Between Curves 347"},{"x":0.08775,"y":0.09093,"w":0.62837,"h":0.03581,"fs":0.03581,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"5.6 Definite Integral Substitutions and the Area Between Curves"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.13689,"w":0.59302,"h":0.07418,"fs":0.01279,"n":5,"k":1,"t":"There are two methods for evaluating a definite integral by substitution. One method is to find an antiderivative using substitution and then to evaluate the definite integral by apply- ing the Evaluation Theorem. The other method extends the process of substitution directly to definite integrals by changing the limits of integration. We apply the new formula intro- duced here to the problem of computing the area between two curves."},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.22733,"w":0.19542,"h":0.01375,"fs":0.01375,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"The Substitution Formula"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.25199,"w":0.59302,"h":0.02814,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"The following formula shows how the limits of integration change when the variable of integration is changed by substitution."},{"x":0.39285,"y":0.30849,"w":0.53247,"h":0.029,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"THEOREM 7—Substitution in Definite Integrals If g′ is continuous on the interval 3a, b4 and ƒ is continuous on the range of g(x) = u, then"},{"x":0.70809,"y":0.3461,"w":0.0191,"h":0.00896,"fs":0.00895,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"g(b)"},{"x":0.55864,"y":0.35024,"w":0.12445,"h":0.02238,"fs":0.01918,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"ƒ(g(x)) # g′(x) dx ="},{"x":0.72714,"y":0.35921,"w":0.05173,"h":0.01281,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"ƒ(u) du."},{"x":0.70073,"y":0.37436,"w":0.0191,"h":0.00896,"fs":0.00895,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"g(a)"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.41176,"w":0.35174,"h":0.01391,"fs":0.01311,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"Proof Let F denote any antiderivative of ƒ. Then,"},{"x":0.73054,"y":0.42795,"w":0.1529,"h":0.02274,"fs":0.01023,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"x=b d dx F(g(x))"},{"x":0.54223,"y":0.43697,"w":0.18586,"h":0.02383,"fs":0.01918,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"ƒ(g(x)) # g′(x) dx = F(g(x))d"},{"x":0.83553,"y":0.45154,"w":0.08165,"h":0.02485,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"= F′(g(x))g′(x) = ƒ(g(x))g′(x)"},{"x":0.73054,"y":0.46151,"w":0.02002,"h":0.00915,"fs":0.0087,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"x=a"},{"x":0.65787,"y":0.4851,"w":0.13748,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"= F(g(b)) - F(g(a))"},{"x":0.71327,"y":0.50564,"w":0.03355,"h":0.00916,"fs":0.0087,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"u=g(b)"},{"x":0.65787,"y":0.50603,"w":0.05295,"h":0.02323,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"= F(u)d"},{"x":0.71327,"y":0.53057,"w":0.03355,"h":0.00916,"fs":0.0087,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"u=g(a)"},{"x":0.69251,"y":0.54682,"w":0.0191,"h":0.00896,"fs":0.00895,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"g(b)"},{"x":0.71157,"y":0.55478,"w":0.20194,"h":0.02302,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"ƒ(u) du. Fundamental Theorem, Part 2"},{"x":0.68515,"y":0.57509,"w":0.0191,"h":0.00896,"fs":0.00895,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"g(a)"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.59764,"w":0.59304,"h":0.05951,"fs":0.01279,"n":4,"k":1,"t":"To use the formula, make the same u-substitution u = g(x) and du = g′(x) dx you would use to evaluate the corresponding indefinite integral. Then integrate the transformed integral with respect to u from the value g(a) (the value of u at x = a) to the value g(b) (the value of u at x = b)."},{"x":0.57482,"y":0.67885,"w":0.10776,"h":0.01795,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"3x22x3 + 1 dx."},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.6823,"w":0.18591,"h":0.01449,"fs":0.01407,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"EXAMPLE 1 Evaluate"},{"x":0.55961,"y":0.69867,"w":0.01286,"h":0.00943,"fs":0.00895,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"-1"},{"x":0.36259,"y":0.71384,"w":0.21695,"h":0.01365,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"Solution We have two choices."},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.73533,"w":0.58895,"h":0.02925,"fs":0.01311,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"Method 1: Transform the integral and evaluate the transformed integral with the trans- formed limits given in Theorem 7."},{"x":0.60306,"y":0.76869,"w":0.19378,"h":0.03994,"fs":0.01023,"n":3,"k":0,"t":"Let u = x3 + 1, du = 3x2 dx. When x = -1, u = 